One source of identification is exclusion restrictions. That is, there exists a variable, such as price, that enters the utility of one product, u¯i​j​t\bar{u}\_{ijt}, but not the other, u¯i,−j,t\bar{u}\_{i,-j,t}. As argued by [Gentzkow, (2007)](https://arxiv.org/html/2412.05794v1#bib.bib18), if the goods are complements (Γ(1,2)>0\Gamma\_{(1,2)}>0), shifting up the utility of good 1 (e.g., by decreasing its price p1p\_{1}) should increase the probability of consuming good 2. In contrast, if Γ(1,2)=0\Gamma\_{(1,2)}=0, the probability of consuming good 2 should remain unchanged. Formalizing Gentzkow’s identification argument, [Fox and Lazzati, (2017)](https://arxiv.org/html/2412.05794v1#bib.bib16) prove that an excluded variable for each product utility u¯i​j​t\bar{u}\_{ijt} with large support (i.e., spanning the real line ℝ\mathbb{R}) provides the separate identification of the bundle effects Γ⁡(1,2)\Gamma{(1,2)} and the joint error distribution F⁡(νi​1​t,νi​2​t)F(\nu\_{i1t},\nu\_{i2t}). Additionally, [Keane, (1992)](https://arxiv.org/html/2412.05794v1#bib.bib31) provides evidence on the role of exclusion restrictions in identifying the covariance parameters in multinomial probit models through Monte Carlo tests and an application to real-world data.

